; Transport frames — domain-independent; every peer speaks these regardless
; of the negotiated capability set, since they carry the connection itself
; rather than any one domain's content. Field layouts transcribed directly
; from Cascade's own BepMessage/Candidate/CandidateKind types, re-expressed
; in CDDL rather than XDR.
;
; Cascade's own `content` domain (BEP block exchange: ClusterConfig, Index,
; IndexUpdate, Request, Response) is deliberately out of scope here — it has
; no analogue in agent-comms and doesn't fit any of wire-mesh's generic
; patterns. It remains Cascade's own extension, pluggable into the
; $frame-variant socket (see frame.cddl) from Cascade's own schema file,
; never part of the core protocol.

ping-frame  = { type: "ping" }
; A bare echo of ping-frame (wire-mesh#181), sent by a hub in reply to a ping it receives -- deliberately not itself a request/response pair with any correlation id, matching ping-frame's own no-id shape: a connection carries at most one outstanding ping/pong exchange's worth of ambiguity at a time, the same tolerance an ordinary ICMP-style liveness probe already accepts. This is what lets a client isolate its own sender-to-hub leg of a relayed round trip: time a bare ping/pong over the same connection a relayed manage-request/response travelled, then subtract that leg from path.trace's own end-to-end RTT (core/management's path:trace, spec/management.cddl) to recover the hub-to-target leg. Deliberately below the manage-command/capability layer entirely, the same reasoning ping-frame and gossip-frame already exempt themselves from webrtc.cddl's own comment on why they carry no comparable receiver-side effect worth gating.
pong-frame  = { type: "pong" }
close-frame = { type: "close", ? reason: tstr }

; The extension tail mirrors token-claims' own `* tstr => any` (tokens.cddl)
; and is the generalised gossip-extension point named in the agent-comms
; migration design's own P4 refinement: a node's presence status, its own
; accept/refuse policy for a claim class (e.g. "does not accept messages
; carrying a valid-until claim"), and any future domain's own gossiped fact
; all ride this one open tail, added once rather than as a series of
; separately-named fields each time a new fact needs advertising. A field
; here is covered by the advert's own signature (see below), so it is
; authentic — it really was asserted by the device the advert names — but it
; is still self-asserted: the signature proves who said it, never that what
; was said is true. A reader treats an unrecognised key the same
; verifier-obligation way an unrecognised value in an open discriminator is
; treated elsewhere in this spec: ignored, never acted on without
; understanding it, never treated as an error.
;
; A key here MUST be domain-qualified as "<domain>/<field>" (e.g.
; "presence/status", not bare "status") — see spec/CONVENTIONS.md's
; gossip-extension-namespacing convention. This is what lets two independent
; applications advertising over the same session coexist without one's
; extension silently shadowing the other's identically-named field. A key
; here MUST also never repeat one of this map's own typed field names
; (device/addresses/snapshot-seconds/identity-key/signature) — an
; implementation MUST reject an attempt to advertise under one of those
; names rather than let it shadow the real field.
;
; Every advert is signed by the device it names, and is worthless without
; that signature. An advert legitimately travels beyond its own author — a
; hub re-broadcasts what it receives, and a gateway forwards adverts for the
; local peers it fronts — so binding an advert to the connection it arrived
; on cannot work: the signature, not the connection, is what ties an advert
; to the device it claims to describe. `identity-key` travels inside the
; signed content exactly as token-claims' own `issuer-key` does, making an
; advert self-certifying: no prior contact with the advertised device, and
; no directory to consult, is needed to check one.
;
; Validity is two checks, both verifier obligations rather than CDDL
; constraints. First, self-certification: sha256(identity-key.public-key)
; MUST equal `device`, the same obligation CONVENTIONS.md's own glossary
; already states for every other embedded identity-key. Second, the
; signature MUST verify under that same embedded key over the signing input
; defined below. A verifier that cannot check both MUST refuse the advert
; rather than apply it, the same fail-closed treatment an unrecognised open
; discriminator already gets.
;
; The signing input is the domain-separation string "wire-mesh/peer-advert/
; v1" followed by one zero byte, concatenated with the canonical CDE
; encoding of this map with the `signature` entry removed and nothing else
; changed. Every other entry is therefore covered, extension tail included,
; so no gossiped fact can be altered, added, or dropped without invalidating
; the signature. Deliberately NOT tokens.cddl's COSE Sig_structure, despite
; that being the reused construction everywhere else a signature appears
; here: an advert's extension tail is an open `* tstr => any` map, so an
; attacker can craft an advert whose encoding also satisfies token-claims,
; and a Sig_structure over it would then be replayable as a capability
; token's own signed payload. The prefix makes the two byte strings disjoint
; by construction — a Sig_structure always begins with the CBOR encoding of
; a 4-element array whose first element is "Signature1", never the ASCII "w"
; this input starts with.
;
; Signatures alone do not stop a connection replaying a victim's genuine,
; correctly-signed advert to draw that device's routed traffic to itself, so
; a hub applies one further receiver obligation when registering which
; connection a device is reachable over. An advert naming a device currently
; registered to a DIFFERENT live connection takes that registration over
; only when its `snapshot-seconds` is strictly greater than the registered
; advert's; an advert from the connection that already holds the
; registration updates it when `snapshot-seconds` is greater than or equal.
; The known limitation this leaves, stated rather than papered over: a
; device that reconnects within the same second while its previous
; connection is still open does not displace that connection until its next
; gossip carries a later second.
peer-advert = {
  device: device-id,
  addresses: [* tstr],      ; "host:port" strings
  snapshot-seconds: int,     ; Unix-seconds snapshot time
  identity-key: identity-key,   ; the advertised device's own key; sha256(public-key) MUST equal `device`
  signature: bstr,           ; over the signing input described above, under identity-key
  * tstr => any,
}
gossip-frame = { type: "gossip", peers: [* peer-advert] }

candidate-kind = "host" / "server-reflexive" / "relayed"
wire-candidate = { address: tstr, kind: candidate-kind, priority: uint }   ; address = "ip:port"
candidates-frame = { type: "candidates", candidates: [* wire-candidate] }

sync-punch-frame = { type: "sync-punch", nonce: uint, deadline-unix-ms: uint }
observed-address-frame = { type: "observed-address", address: tstr }

; Relay: when two peers cannot connect directly, a relay carries their
; traffic as an opaque, unreadable byte pipe. The relay never holds the keys
; to decrypt what it forwards — relay-data-frame's payload is ciphertext
; established one layer above the transport, not something this frame
; interprets. Who is allowed to use a given relay as a service is
; deliberately not part of this shape: relay-connect-frame carries no
; capability-token field, so an operator that wants to gate relay access
; (rather than run an open relay) enforces it the same way any other
; behaviour is gated in this schema — an ordinary core/management verb
; checked before honouring subsequent relay frames on that connection, not a
; constraint this frame's own fields express.
relay-offer-frame   = { type: "relay-offer", addresses: [* tstr] }
relay-connect-frame = { type: "relay-connect", target-device: device-id }
; Multiplexing: a connection may hold more than one relay pairing at once (a
; peer fanning a message out to several members of a group, all of whom are
; only reachable through this same hub). `to-device` names which pairing an
; outbound payload belongs to, and `from-device` names which pairing an
; inbound one arrived on -- the same demultiplexing `relay-inbound` already
; does once per pairing, now carried per frame so it stays unambiguous when a
; connection holds several. Directional obligations, not something CDDL
; itself can enforce (the same idiom as coordinator-frame's tiebreak rule
; above): a peer sets `to-device` and MUST NOT set `from-device`; a hub
; overwrites `from-device` with the sending peer's own gossiped device-id on
; every frame it forwards, and strips `to-device`. A connection holding
; exactly one pairing may omit `to-device` entirely, which is what every
; pre-multiplexing peer does.
relay-data-frame    = { type: "relay-data", payload: bstr, ? to-device: device-id, ? from-device: device-id }
relay-inbound-frame = { type: "relay-inbound", source-device: device-id }

; Coordinator election — a lightweight, gossiped claim to the introduction/
; rendezvous role among whichever peers are presently reachable (there is no
; membership list to scope this to: "the mesh" is the live connected
; component of the transport graph, nothing more, so this frame is simply
; gossiped the same way peer-advert already is, and reaches exactly the
; peers that are reachable). `term` is a monotonically increasing epoch a
; peer raises when claiming the role; a higher term always supersedes a
; lower one, and two simultaneous claims at equal terms are broken by lowest
; device-id — a verifier/receiver obligation, not something CDDL itself can
; enforce, the same way capability-token delegation's narrowing rule is.
; This gives the rendezvous role the same crash-recovery property a
; port-race coordinator already has (any live peer can take over) without
; relying on OS-level port contention to do the electing.
coordinator-frame = { type: "coordinator", term: uint, coordinator: device-id, ? capacity-hint: uint }
